Want to take a shot at using TOSTER to check equivalence
- I want to determine what defensible equivalence bounds would be empirically by 
  comparing two highly similar datasets, but for now I will be somewhat conservative 
  and use 10% difference as my equivalence bounds. 
- I am going to compare my slope + SE from the linear model, against a hypothetical
  slope from data that is perfectly correlated, so a slope = 1, with sd = 0, with the 
  same number of datapoints. 
- I think this is a defensible way of doing this; ideally I'd have a slope from two 
  datasets that are practically equivalent (i.e. technical replicates from same sample)
  but, a perfect correlation should be more strict rather than less, so I think this is 
  ok. 
- After running the TOST, it appears that the slope is practically equivalent to 1, within
  10% difference; so look's good (as we suspected)

